bohmian-velocity-26a97dcb.interpretation:
The formula $\mathbf{v} =  rac{\hbar}{m} \operatorname{Im}\left( rac{
abla \psi}{\psi}ight)$ defines the velocity vector $\mathbf{v}$ of a quantum particle with mass $m$. The term $\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant. The function $\psi$ is the complex-valued wave function of the particle, which is a solution to the Schrödinger equation and contains all information about the quantum state of the system. The expression $ rac{
abla \psi}{\psi}$ is the logarithmic derivative of the wave function, a complex vector quantity. The operator $\operatorname{Im}(\cdot)$ extracts the imaginary part of its argument. By expressing the wave function in polar form, $\psi = R e^{iS/\hbar}$, where $R(\mathbf{x}, t)$ is the real amplitude and $S(\mathbf{x}, t)$ is the real phase, the logarithmic derivative becomes $ rac{
abla \psi}{\psi} =  rac{
abla R}{R} +  rac{i}{\hbar}
abla S$. Taking the imaginary part yields $\operatorname{Im}\left( rac{
abla \psi}{\psi}ight) =  rac{
abla S}{\hbar}$. Substituting this back into the velocity equation gives the equivalent form $\mathbf{v} =  rac{\hbar}{m} \left( rac{
abla S}{\hbar}ight) =  rac{
abla S}{m}$. This explicitly shows that the particle's velocity is determined by the gradient of the phase $S$ of the wave function, which plays a role analogous to the action in the Hamilton-Jacobi formulation of classical mechanics.
